Transportation food housing these are  some of the biggest categories in the  Consumer Price Index Market Basket one  of the measures used to track inflation  and here's how they're weighted you can  see housing or what the BLS calls  shelter makes up about a third of the  Market Basket which means it can be a  big factor in either driving or  tempering inflation I think the shelter  shelter inflation is going to remain  high for some time you know we're  looking for it to come down but it's not  exactly clear when that will happen but  it's also one of the hardest numbers to  measure and as you can see here it can  keep Rising even when inflation in other  items begins to slow down that can make  things complicated for the fed and  policy makers if they're trying to push  inflation down as they are right now  here's why housing is one of the  trickiest items in the basket to measure  and why it holds so much weight in the  overall index  when the BLS measures for shelter it's  measuring for the service that a housing  unit provides its occupants or more  simply the cost or value of your home  you think of how much money you spend  every month a big chunk of that goes to  your rent or your mortgage and that's  the case for households across the  country so if it's a big part of your  monthly spending it's going to be a big  part of the overall inflation  calculation the index includes changes  in prices for four different categories  but these two are the most important  rent and something called owner's  equivalent rent let's look at this one  first on the surface it's pretty  self-explanatory it tracks prices people  pay for rent while it's less volatile  than home prices measuring for rent  poses some challenges for the BLS  because it's sticky that means it  changes infrequently since most prices  are locked in place throughout a lease  term but that sticky price means the BLS  has to gather data for it differently  than it does for other categories data  for things like food and transportation  are gathered every month or two but it's  only gathered for rent every six sample  areas called panels report on a  staggered schedule so one might report  prices in January and July while in  other reports in February and August  that means that changes in rent might  not trickle into the bls's data for  months down the line what the BLS is  trying to do is it's trying to capture  both the people who are in the market  for a new apartment who are signing new  leases and the people who are in their  existing lease who are paying the same  price they're paying the last month so  WLS is trying to get a picture of the  overall Market sort of every renter  according to a study by the Dallas fed  changes in the rental market can proceed  changes in the shelter index by about a  year you have to wait for enough people  to sign new leases or to renew their  leases for those price increases to  actually show up but this isn't even the  trickiest part the other even bigger  slice of the index is called owner's  equivalent rent or oer this is the price  an owner would pay to rent a comparable  home  the BLS doesn't Factor house prices into  the CPI so they don't show up directly  in this that's because it considers home  ownership and investment rather than a  consumable good or service like food or  airplane tickets so instead the BLS  assigns owned homes an equivalent rent  based on the average rental prices in  the area it's an estimate so rental  prices can cause a lag and oer can be  somewhat imprecise and you can see that  that can make the shelter index look  really different from what's actually  happening in the housing market that can  make a difference in inflation numbers  because as you can see here shelter is  taking up an increasing share of the  overall CPI  here's Douglas Holtz Eakin former  director of the Congressional budget  office on the importance of bringing  shelter inflation down If the Fed wants  to hit a two percent Target and and  shelter inflation's at six percent  everything else has to be zero that's  not going to happen so the FED needs to  bring down shelter price inflation and  it will increasingly raise rates and  tighten Financial conditions until it  does this pressure means the shelter  index is lag can make things complicated  for fed officials we're looking to  affect overall inflation month to month  the last thing they want to see right  now is inflation going up I mean they've  already put in place a number of rate  increases they have more planned so  they're really looking for signs that  inflation is coming down and this is  pushing in the opposite direction even  if it's you know likely to be temporary  fed Governor Christopher Waller said in  October that while housing inflation  should slow increases in broader  interest rates should help moderate  demand and damp inflation in other  sectors in the meantime though there  isn't a specific date that the shelter  index could start to cool economists  expect it to start to come down anytime  between the end of the year and mid-2023  but it will keep adding to inflation  even as the housing market settles down  [Music]
 

